\description{
Simulates the joint dynamics of abundances and mean traits of a basal resource (\code{R}), a
consumer (\code{N}) and a predator (\code{P}), the three-trophic-level model of chapter 3 of McPeek
(2017). Each level has one quantitative trait whose mean changes at a rate proportional to
the selection gradient, the slope of the logarithm of mean fitness against the trait at the
mean (equation 3.7, after Lande 1982 and Iwasa et al. 1991 as cited there):
\eqn{d\bar z_i / dt = V_i \, \partial \ln W_i / \partial z_i}, where \eqn{V_i} is the
additive genetic variance. Abundances follow the per capita growth rates of equations 3.9
and 3.20: logistic growth of the resource, Holling type II feeding by the consumer on the
resource and by the predator on the consumer and, with \code{omnivory = TRUE}, on the resource
too, with density-dependent death at every level. The intrinsic rates are quadratic in the
traits and the attack coefficients take one of the three forms of \code{\link[=food_web_params]{food_web_params()}}.
}
\details{
The fitness of an individual with phenotype \code{z} is evaluated with every other individual at
the mean trait (equations 3.8 and 3.20): the satiation of a consumer depends on its own
trait, so the trait of an individual enters both the numerator and the denominator of the
functional responses of the links where it feeds, but only the numerator of the links where
it is eaten. Selection gradients are central differences of this fitness with respect to
\code{z} at the mean, so the three attack forms share one implementation. Abundances and means
are integrated together by a fourth-order Runge-Kutta method with a fixed step \code{dt}, which
must be short against the fastest rate of the system (about a tenth of \eqn{1 / c_0}{1 / c0}
or less):
halving \code{dt} should leave the result unchanged, and a run whose state stops being finite
or whose abundances overshoot below zero stops with an error.

A level with abundance zero is absent: its trait is held and its rate of change is zero.
Levels can be added during the run with \code{introductions}, to follow the burst of evolution
after an invasion and its deceleration towards equilibrium (figures 3.2 and 3.3 of the
book), and to compare the trait of the resource with and without the predator (figure
3.11). Abundances that fall below \code{extinction} are set to zero. \code{\link[=plot.food_web]{plot.food_web()}} draws
the trajectories and \code{\link[=animate_food_web]{animate_food_web()}} turns them into a GIF, such as this one, in which
a consumer invades the resource and a predator arrives at time 6000 (parameters of the
Gaussian panel of figure 3.11, made by \code{data-raw/food_web_gif.R}):

\if{html}{\figure{food_web_dynamics.gif}{options: width="600" alt="Animated abundances, mean traits and rates of trait change of a resource, a consumer and a predator"}}

Two details of equation 3.20 are read as follows. Its loss term of the resource to the
predator carries the conversion efficiency \code{w}, which the matching selection gradient of
the resource, the consumer's loss term and equation 3.9 do not; the implementation leaves
\code{w} out of the losses of prey, as in the other equations. The attack coefficient of the
predator on the consumer is written there with the trait of the resource; it is taken as a
function of the traits of the consumer and the predator, as box 3.1 defines it; with
this reading the three-level panel A of figure 3.11 is reproduced closely.

For the resource and the consumer, the implementation matches the MATLAB code of the
resource-consumer models that McPeek (2017, \emph{The American Naturalist}) published as
supplementary material: the same fitness functions, functional responses and selection
gradients, and trajectories of figure 2 of that article (figure 3.2 of the book) within
1e-4 of the author's \code{ode45} integration in continuous time, where one "iteration" of the
book is one unit of time. That code floors the mean traits at zero (\code{trait_floor}) and has
no extinction threshold. It has no predator, so the predator level rests on the equations
of the book alone.
